592186426071 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426047 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426071 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426054 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426071 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426064 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426071 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426071 1077 "selection" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426071 1078 #edb/ref 17592186426064 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426071 1079 #uuid "fec9750c-eaeb-571d-b465-6fa1ab05e88d" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426066 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426072 1069 "q-139978" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426072 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ has no inverse\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero\n\nIII. $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426072 1072 #uuid "251cfe7e-474d-59b2-8ccf-101401daf068" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426072 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $det(A)=0,$ the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero.* $\\,\\times $\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn,  means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis so the dimension must be greater than zero.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))\\ne 5,$ which means that $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426069 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426073 1069 "q-141191" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426073 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426073 1072 #uuid "33a20d40-e8ca-5cc3-bf01-9f700d36b547" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426073 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $0.$" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426068 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426074 1069 "q-142468" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426074 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426074 1072 #uuid "71223fcf-7212-5023-82ea-b55cb18fa0b2" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426074 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\{0\\}.$" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426067 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426075 1069 "q-142471" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426075 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426075 1072 #uuid "937a6bb1-f955-5ee0-bbd6-d97686bceb6d" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426075 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $0.$" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426071 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426076 1069 "q-142367" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426076 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$  is  invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426076 1072 #uuid "9fbccd10-1c43-51a4-ad8b-36063e11427d" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426076 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426070 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426077 1069 "q-139977" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426077 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)<4$" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426077 1072 #uuid "a0f098cb-e297-54fe-a435-e8a16d43aa06" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true] [17592186426077 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534571 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0c95950f-0823-5370-bdc6-01bb92a952d1" 17592186426033 "answer-0d6fe511-51db-512e-9712-3fc